sh at the end of the supplement to match.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-614-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7d6c600b-1a46-43c0-b390-c3dc1d13275a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959339,"updateDate":1691007959339}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959339, 1691007959339),
    ($SATQ$42cc9236$SATQ$, $SATQ$ba43ba37-2bb4-4501-96e8-9cc10f4f776b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$3aeb980d-bc12-41b4-bbf4-8204b3720896$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>If you try on one of artist Nick Cave&rsquo;s signature Soundsuits, you can expect to swish, rustle, or clang every time you move. Cave makes his suits out of found objects, everything from ceramic birds to broken record players. He carefully considers the sound an object makes before using <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in a suit.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2fcdd5bc-2c2c-40b0-94f3-33c52e06f683","content":"<p>this</p>"},{"id":"824d2a9a-cca8-451e-93e6-9520934a722f","content":"<p>that</p>"},{"id":"c9fa75bf-5fff-4d07-aff2-d7d31510ebf4","content":"<p>these</p>"},{"id":"50175419-76ba-4786-b35d-09c1938f2c0f","content":"<p>it</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["50175419-76ba-4786-b35d-09c1938f2c0f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested here is pronoun-antecedent agreement. The singular pronoun "it" agrees in number with the singular antecedent "object."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The singular pronoun "this" is used to refer to a specific thing, but here there is no specific object being referred to. Instead, the sentence is referring back to a hypothetical object that Cave might use in a Soundsuit. Choice B is incorrect. The singular pronoun "that" is used to refer to a specific thing, but here there is no specific object being referred to. Instead, the sentence is referring back to a hypothetical object that Cave might use in a Soundsuit. Choice C is incorrect because the plural pronoun "these" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent "object."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-722-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$fdc10fc4-4538-4018-b962-9170bc7bad8f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"42cc9236","external_id":"ba43ba37-2bb4-4501-96e8-9cc10f4f776b","vaultid":"3aeb980d-bc12-41b4-bbf4-8204b3720896","uId":"cc9ef3d6-878e-40b8-bd0d-7fd3d7190cbd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>If you try on one of artist Nick Cave&rsquo;s signature Soundsuits, you can expect to swish, rustle, or clang every time you move. Cave makes his suits out of found objects, everything from ceramic birds to broken record players. He carefully considers the sound an object makes before using <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in a suit.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2fcdd5bc-2c2c-40b0-94f3-33c52e06f683","content":"<p>this</p>"},{"id":"824d2a9a-cca8-451e-93e6-9520934a722f","content":"<p>that</p>"},{"id":"c9fa75bf-5fff-4d07-aff2-d7d31510ebf4","content":"<p>these</p>"},{"id":"50175419-76ba-4786-b35d-09c1938f2c0f","content":"<p>it</p>"}],"keys":["50175419-76ba-4786-b35d-09c1938f2c0f"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested here is pronoun-antecedent agreement. The singular pronoun \"it\" agrees in number with the singular antecedent \"object.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The singular pronoun \"this\" is used to refer to a specific thing, but here there is no specific object being referred to. Instead, the sentence is referring back to a hypothetical object that Cave might use in a Soundsuit. Choice B is incorrect. The singular pronoun \"that\" is used to refer to a specific thing, but here there is no specific object being referred to. Instead, the sentence is referring back to a hypothetical object that Cave might use in a Soundsuit. Choice C is incorrect because the plural pronoun \"these\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent \"object.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-722-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"fdc10fc4-4538-4018-b962-9170bc7bad8f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959348,"updateDate":1691007959348}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959348, 1691007959348),
    ($SATQ$430d929a$SATQ$, $SATQ$7bfeb66b-6a87-475b-adf5-180cccf63991$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1bf6f778-7169-488c-b5e3-eee5e90c80f7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>British scientists James Watson and Francis Crick won the Nobel Prize in part for their 1953 paper announcing the double helix structure of DNA, but it is misleading to say that Watson and Crick discovered the double helix. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> findings were based on a famous X-ray image of DNA fibers, &ldquo;Photo 51,&rdquo; developed by X-ray crystallographer Rosalind Franklin and her graduate student Raymond Gosling.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"63689ecf-33cb-4bc8-a80d-4e8bd816bdb9","content":"<p>They&rsquo;re</p>"},{"id":"f5ad814d-966c-4db1-8e99-de01b202a619","content":"<p>It&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"bfa96f80-1f8c-48b6-b08e-42410f0cf7de","content":"<p>Their</p>"},{"id":"67747f12-cdb5-41bd-825e-b6428ee2de89","content":"<p>Its</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["bfa96f80-1f8c-48b6-b08e-42410f0cf7de"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of possessive determiners. The plural possessive determiner &ldquo;their&rdquo; agrees in number with the plural conjoined noun phrase &ldquo;Watson and Crick&rdquo; and thus indicates that the findings were those of Watson and Crick. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;they&rsquo;re&rdquo; is the contraction for &ldquo;they are,&rdquo; not a possessive determiner. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;it&rsquo;s&rdquo; is the contraction for &ldquo;it is&rdquo; or &ldquo;it has,&rdquo; not a possessive determiner. Choice D is incorrect because&nbsp;the singular possessive determiner &ldquo;its&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural conjoined noun phrase &ldquo;Watson and Crick.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-635-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c8cfb123-dcef-4262-85a3-f6bf1763e4f9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"430d929a","external_id":"7bfeb66b-6a87-475b-adf5-180cccf63991","vaultid":"1bf6f778-7169-488c-b5e3-eee5e90c80f7","uId":"4c784af3-014c-4678-a895-db3116acc19a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>British scientists James Watson and Francis Crick won the Nobel Prize in part for their 1953 paper announcing the double helix structure of DNA, but it is misleading to say that Watson and Crick discovered the double helix. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> findings were based on a famous X-ray image of DNA fibers, &ldquo;Photo 51,&rdquo; developed by X-ray crystallographer Rosalind Franklin and her graduate student Raymond Gosling.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"63689ecf-33cb-4bc8-a80d-4e8bd816bdb9","content":"<p>They&rsquo;re</p>"},{"id":"f5ad814d-966c-4db1-8e99-de01b202a619","content":"<p>It&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"bfa96f80-1f8c-48b6-b08e-42410f0cf7de","content":"<p>Their</p>"},{"id":"67747f12-cdb5-41bd-825e-b6428ee2de89","content":"<p>Its</p>"}],"keys":["bfa96f80-1f8c-48b6-b08e-42410f0cf7de"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of possessive determiners. The plural possessive determiner &ldquo;their&rdquo; agrees in number with the plural conjoined noun phrase &ldquo;Watson and Crick&rdquo; and thus indicates that the findings were those of Watson and Crick. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;they&rsquo;re&rdquo; is the contraction for &ldquo;they are,&rdquo; not a possessive determiner. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;it&rsquo;s&rdquo; is the contraction for &ldquo;it is&rdquo; or &ldquo;it has,&rdquo; not a possessive determiner. Choice D is incorrect because&nbsp;the singular possessive determiner &ldquo;its&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural conjoined noun phrase &ldquo;Watson and Crick.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-635-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c8cfb123-dcef-4262-85a3-f6bf1763e4f9","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959340,"updateDate":1691007959340}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959340, 1691007959340),
    ($SATQ$432b1ede$SATQ$, $SATQ$e0b53c52-f897-4582-b7de-f6e211e87ff7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$07401630-a4f5-47e0-9270-dcff80c003d0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The forty-seven geothermal springs of Arkansas&rsquo; Hot Springs National Park are sourced via a process known as natural groundwater recharge, in which rainwater percolates downward through the earth&mdash;in this case, the porous rocks of the hills around Hot <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> collect in a subterranean basin.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"133fe346-c482-49fc-bd04-0f3484b879d0","content":"<p>Springs to</p>"},{"id":"ba81b3b9-9814-4ea1-a0de-35a199488baa","content":"<p>Springs: to</p>"},{"id":"ecbcd903-1ae8-4610-bfee-d6aee50a7a2c","content":"<p>Springs&mdash;to</p>"},{"id":"0d6d7834-4656-4a3d-bfdb-4aa4c16402a3","content":"<p>Springs, to</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ecbcd903-1ae8-4610-bfee-d6aee50a7a2c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of a supplementary element within a sentence. The dash after &ldquo;Springs&rdquo; pairs with the dash after &ldquo;earth&rdquo; to separate the supplementary element &ldquo;in this case, the porous rocks of the hills around Hot Springs&rdquo; from the rest of the sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice B is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be paired with a dash in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because a comma can&rsquo;t be paired with a dash in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-705-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c059cab8-190c-4565-baf2-f72dec249405$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"432b1ede","external_id":"e0b53c52-f897-4582-b7de-f6e211e87ff7","vaultid":"07401630-a4f5-47e0-9270-dcff80c003d0","uId":"817426e0-a390-4f88-a582-87a7373dd64c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The forty-seven geothermal springs of Arkansas&rsquo; Hot Springs National Park are sourced via a process known as natural groundwater recharge, in which rainwater percolates downward through the earth&mdash;in this case, the porous rocks of the hills around Hot <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> collect in a subterranean basin.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"133fe346-c482-49fc-bd04-0f3484b879d0","content":"<p>Springs to</p>"},{"id":"ba81b3b9-9814-4ea1-a0de-35a199488baa","content":"<p>Springs: to</p>"},{"id":"ecbcd903-1ae8-4610-bfee-d6aee50a7a2c","content":"<p>Springs&mdash;to</p>"},{"id":"0d6d7834-4656-4a3d-bfdb-4aa4c16402a3","content":"<p>Springs, to</p>"}],"keys":["ecbcd903-1ae8-4610-bfee-d6aee50a7a2c"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of a supplementary element within a sentence. The dash after &ldquo;Springs&rdquo; pairs with the dash after &ldquo;earth&rdquo; to separate the supplementary element &ldquo;in this case, the porous rocks of the hills around Hot Springs&rdquo; from the rest of the sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice B is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be paired with a dash in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because a comma can&rsquo;t be paired with a dash in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-705-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c059cab8-190c-4565-baf2-f72dec249405","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959347,"updateDate":1691007959347}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959347, 1691007959347),
    ($SATQ$435809d8$SATQ$, $SATQ$758fbc90-f07d-463d-9e8a-30c83122b3ce$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>On March 23, 2021, a gust of wind wreaked havoc on global trade. <em>Ever Given</em>, an international shipping container vessel, became lodged in Egypt&rsquo;s Suez Canal, a major shipping route between Europe and Asia. The vessel took six days to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> it&rsquo;s as heavy as two thousand blue whales when fully loaded.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b4da1149-fa45-4439-b69a-7a7289152089","content":"<p>dislodge in part due to its sheer size,</p>"},{"id":"9a3fb71d-d02e-41fa-a996-687070c6e6e8","content":"<p>dislodge, in part due to its sheer size:</p>"},{"id":"dbcd47f7-633f-48ca-8f13-166db0f5f4c7","content":"<p>dislodge, in part due to its sheer size,</p>"},{"id":"101354ea-f922-4425-b517-1c2282a73581","content":"<p>dislodge, in part, due to its sheer size</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9a3fb71d-d02e-41fa-a996-687070c6e6e8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between main clauses and a supplementary element. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the main clause ("The vessel took six days to dislodge") and the supplementary element ("in part due to its sheer size") that provides additional information on why the vessel was difficult to dislodge. Additionally, this choice correctly uses a colon to introduce another main clause that describes the vessel&rsquo;s size ("it&rsquo;s as heavy as two thousand blue whales when fully loaded").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between two main clauses ("The vessel&hellip;size" and "it&rsquo;s&hellip;loaded"). Additionally, it fails to mark the boundary between the main clause ("The vessel took six days to dislodge") and the supplementary element ("in part due to its sheer size"). Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between two main clauses ("The vessel&hellip;size" and "it&rsquo;s&hellip;loaded"). Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses ("The vessel&hellip;size" and "it&rsquo;s&hellip;loaded") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"435809d8","external_id":"758fbc90-f07d-463d-9e8a-30c83122b3ce","vaultid":null,"uId":"2b50620d-3025-4978-9e05-7b4f3d092cb2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>On March 23, 2021, a gust of wind wreaked havoc on global trade. <em>Ever Given</em>, an international shipping container vessel, became lodged in Egypt&rsquo;s Suez Canal, a major shipping route between Europe and Asia. The vessel took six days to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> it&rsquo;s as heavy as two thousand blue whales when fully loaded.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b4da1149-fa45-4439-b69a-7a7289152089","content":"<p>dislodge in part due to its sheer size,</p>"},{"id":"9a3fb71d-d02e-41fa-a996-687070c6e6e8","content":"<p>dislodge, in part due to its sheer size:</p>"},{"id":"dbcd47f7-633f-48ca-8f13-166db0f5f4c7","content":"<p>dislodge, in part due to its sheer size,</p>"},{"id":"101354ea-f922-4425-b517-1c2282a73581","content":"<p>dislodge, in part, due to its sheer size</p>"}],"keys":["9a3fb71d-d02e-41fa-a996-687070c6e6e8"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between main clauses and a supplementary element. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the main clause (\"The vessel took six days to dislodge\") and the supplementary element (\"in part due to its sheer size\") that provides additional information on why the vessel was difficult to dislodge. Additionally, this choice correctly uses a colon to introduce another main clause that describes the vessel&rsquo;s size (\"it&rsquo;s as heavy as two thousand blue whales when fully loaded\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between two main clauses (\"The vessel&hellip;size\" and \"it&rsquo;s&hellip;loaded\"). Additionally, it fails to mark the boundary between the main clause (\"The vessel took six days to dislodge\") and the supplementary element (\"in part due to its sheer size\"). Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between two main clauses (\"The vessel&hellip;size\" and \"it&rsquo;s&hellip;loaded\"). Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses (\"The vessel&hellip;size\" and \"it&rsquo;s&hellip;loaded\") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323049,"updateDate":1730147323049}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323049, 1730147323049),
    ($SATQ$4565a53c$SATQ$, $SATQ$9ec6e792-f2d9-4e2e-92ec-c9b165923558$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$bf17a212-7bde-4adb-97f0-887f90cad5c5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Luc&iacute;a Michel of the University of Chile observed that alkaline soils contain an insoluble form of iron that blueberry plants cannot absorb, thus inhibiting blueberry growth. If these plants were grown in alkaline soil alongside grasses that aid in iron solubilization, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Michel was determined to find out.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fa36a957-5d05-4ba2-99f2-fc8a843f229f","content":"<p>could the blueberries thrive.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"7116c1cb-7369-4742-a4ee-dbb4dbabdad6","content":"<p>the blueberries could thrive.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"bb599286-38d9-4b42-b1d5-b0f74350d97c","content":"<p>the blueberries could thrive?</p>"},{"id":"f2546507-3902-4a4a-9b34-b43cbb0348d3","content":"<p>could the blueberries thrive?</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f2546507-3902-4a4a-9b34-b43cbb0348d3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is end-of-sentence punctuation. This choice correctly uses a question mark to punctuate the interrogative clause &ldquo;could the blueberries thrive,&rdquo; which asks a direct question at the end of the sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a period can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate an interrogative clause, such as &ldquo;could the blueberries thrive,&rdquo; at the end of a sentence. Choice B is incorrect because the context requires an interrogative clause. The declarative clause &ldquo;the blueberries could thrive&rdquo; incorrectly ind