e punctuation.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323050,"updateDate":1730147323050}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323050, 1730147323050),
    ($SATQ$4a90a978$SATQ$, $SATQ$c94d0117-bc9c-470c-9921-5ab8d5bc08df$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1312b3b1-ec3f-4e8f-abd1-6152a868b0a6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 1990, California native and researcher Ellen Ochoa left her position as chief of the Intelligent Systems Technology Branch at a NASA research center <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the space agency&rsquo;s astronaut training program.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4f5568d9-5b5d-42bb-bf61-c77f5586b9e9","content":"<p>to join</p>"},{"id":"4edbd910-3b9a-4c42-b8df-2b1db1dca78d","content":"<p>is joining</p>"},{"id":"87979613-f5c3-429b-9aec-3ca6f9618bc8","content":"<p>joined</p>"},{"id":"ac38c86d-eadd-4307-9f9e-e8473d56182b","content":"<p>joins</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4f5568d9-5b5d-42bb-bf61-c77f5586b9e9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of nonfinite verb forms in a sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive verb "to join" is correctly used to form a subordinate clause that expresses why Ochoa left her position (to join the training program).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the finite verb "is joining" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to indicate Ochoa&rsquo;s action of joining the training program. A conjunction such as "and" would be needed to coordinate "is joining" with the previous finite verb, "left." Choice C is incorrect because the finite verb "joined" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to indicate Ochoa&rsquo;s action of joining the training program. A conjunction such as "and" would be needed to coordinate "joined" with the previous finite verb, "left." Choice D is incorrect because the finite verb "joins" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to indicate Ochoa&rsquo;s action of joining the training program. A conjunction such as "and" would be needed to coordinate "joins" with the previous finite verb, "left."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-E002$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$d93d4408-11d1-4d9e-be01-a965c3baa2f3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4a90a978","external_id":"c94d0117-bc9c-470c-9921-5ab8d5bc08df","vaultid":"1312b3b1-ec3f-4e8f-abd1-6152a868b0a6","uId":"e34e684d-fbe4-4caf-a4a3-26ac784e8cab","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 1990, California native and researcher Ellen Ochoa left her position as chief of the Intelligent Systems Technology Branch at a NASA research center <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the space agency&rsquo;s astronaut training program.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4f5568d9-5b5d-42bb-bf61-c77f5586b9e9","content":"<p>to join</p>"},{"id":"4edbd910-3b9a-4c42-b8df-2b1db1dca78d","content":"<p>is joining</p>"},{"id":"87979613-f5c3-429b-9aec-3ca6f9618bc8","content":"<p>joined</p>"},{"id":"ac38c86d-eadd-4307-9f9e-e8473d56182b","content":"<p>joins</p>"}],"keys":["4f5568d9-5b5d-42bb-bf61-c77f5586b9e9"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of nonfinite verb forms in a sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive verb \"to join\" is correctly used to form a subordinate clause that expresses why Ochoa left her position (to join the training program).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the finite verb \"is joining\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to indicate Ochoa&rsquo;s action of joining the training program. A conjunction such as \"and\" would be needed to coordinate \"is joining\" with the previous finite verb, \"left.\" Choice C is incorrect because the finite verb \"joined\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to indicate Ochoa&rsquo;s action of joining the training program. A conjunction such as \"and\" would be needed to coordinate \"joined\" with the previous finite verb, \"left.\" Choice D is incorrect because the finite verb \"joins\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to indicate Ochoa&rsquo;s action of joining the training program. A conjunction such as \"and\" would be needed to coordinate \"joins\" with the previous finite verb, \"left.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-FSS-E002","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"d93d4408-11d1-4d9e-be01-a965c3baa2f3","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959366,"updateDate":1691007959366}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959366, 1691007959366),
    ($SATQ$4aa28ac3$SATQ$, $SATQ$e0a47e80-b53e-4a80-a4c7-7e34a1a2ff52$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Nowadays, tug-of-war is usually seen as an informal game one might play at a picnic or in gym class. Surprisingly, the Olympic committee once decided <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> tug-of-war as an official Olympic event! Nations competed in the event at the Olympic Games from 1900 to 1920.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bbdada05-1160-48c2-b814-9936ad1d8d12","content":"<p>included</p>"},{"id":"6fa5eefe-c09f-41f8-bef7-7a7a54019b35","content":"<p>including</p>"},{"id":"0d9a9189-8b19-4e5c-a2a2-a9d8502ca63e","content":"<p>include</p>"},{"id":"425f374d-a177-4ea9-b7ec-db78e8fa86c9","content":"<p>to include</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["425f374d-a177-4ea9-b7ec-db78e8fa86c9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive verb "to include" is correctly used to form a subordinate clause that indicates what the Olympic committee decided (to include tug-of-war as an Olympic event).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite verb "included" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what the Olympic committee decided. Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle "including" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what the Olympic committee decided. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite verb "include" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what the Olympic committee decided.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4aa28ac3","external_id":"e0a47e80-b53e-4a80-a4c7-7e34a1a2ff52","vaultid":null,"uId":"289f1c13-7cd1-49ab-b068-dce9f70dc1b8","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Nowadays, tug-of-war is usually seen as an informal game one might play at a picnic or in gym class. Surprisingly, the Olympic committee once decided <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> tug-of-war as an official Olympic event! Nations competed in the event at the Olympic Games from 1900 to 1920.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bbdada05-1160-48c2-b814-9936ad1d8d12","content":"<p>included</p>"},{"id":"6fa5eefe-c09f-41f8-bef7-7a7a54019b35","content":"<p>including</p>"},{"id":"0d9a9189-8b19-4e5c-a2a2-a9d8502ca63e","content":"<p>include</p>"},{"id":"425f374d-a177-4ea9-b7ec-db78e8fa86c9","content":"<p>to include</p>"}],"keys":["425f374d-a177-4ea9-b7ec-db78e8fa86c9"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive verb \"to include\" is correctly used to form a subordinate clause that indicates what the Olympic committee decided (to include tug-of-war as an Olympic event).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite verb \"included\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what the Olympic committee decided. Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle \"including\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what the Olympic committee decided. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite verb \"include\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a subordinate clause that indicates what the Olympic committee decided.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669214,"updateDate":1743430669214}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669214, 1743430669214),
    ($SATQ$4b0c7b62$SATQ$, $SATQ$002fb221-07c6-4406-a00c-ed57339ea78c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The algaita is a double reed wind instrument from West Africa. The reed of a wind instrument is the mouthpiece <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> A double reed contains two pieces of cane that vibrate and produce sound as air passes between them.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ded3cc27-dd60-44e1-912e-1b49d9f42258","content":"<p>where sound is made?</p>"},{"id":"78968170-65e2-4474-9c42-d244e5fc0aa2","content":"<p>where is sound made.</p>"},{"id":"4401928d-2800-409b-baf3-475cbaac306e","content":"<p>where sound is made.</p>"},{"id":"2bee7168-0da1-4daf-8bfc-ef1cc5b1cfde","content":"<p>where is sound made?</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4401928d-2800-409b-baf3-475cbaac306e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is end-of-sentence punctuation. This choice correctly uses a period to punctuate a declarative sentence ("the reed of a wind instrument is the mouthpiece") that ends with a prepositional phrase ("where sound is made").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. It&rsquo;s unconventional to use a question mark to punctuate a declarative sentence. Choice B is incorrect. The structure requires that the sentence continue as a declarative clause, not end with an interrogative clause. Choice D is incorrect. The structure requires that the sentence continue as a declarative clause and end with a period, not end with an interrogative clause and a question mark.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4b0c7b62","external_id":